WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.486
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.791 --> 00:00:06.290
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Hello, everybody.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:09.620
Thanks a lot for
coming to our talk.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.790
I'm Guillaume Laforge.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:13.495
I'm a Developer Advocate
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:13.495 --> 00:00:14.870
BRAD ABRAMS: And
I'm Brad Abrams.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.680
I'm a Product Manager on
the Google Assistant Team.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.450
And we're going to
talk a little bit

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.090
about how to extend the
Assistant with actions

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:24.380
on Google.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.060
So when Sundar announced the
Assistant at Iowa last year,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.160
it was for one purpose.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.260
And that was to build a
conversational interface

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:34.760
to Google.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.670
So we wanted that
conversational interface

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:46.230
to be available
everywhere that users are.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.870
So we want that conversational
interface to be there

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.925
in your messaging applications.

00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:53.490
So when you're talking
to your friends

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.590
about where to go out to
eat, the Google Assistant

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.720
is there to help you
pick a restaurant.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.730
We want it to be
available in your pocket

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.190
when you're trying
to figure out how

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.780
to get there, full
access to everything

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.180
you can do on your phone.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.520
And of course, we want the
Google Assistant to be there

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.860
in your home when you're kicking
back, listening to some jazz,

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.640
or if you need to read
a recipe or set a timer,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.860
we want the Assistant
to be there too.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.060
And of course, this
is just the beginning.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.640
We actually want
the Assistant to be

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.200
available in all
kinds of devices,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.150
whether that's
dishwashers or tractors.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.520
So how do we think
about the Assistant?

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.590
The way we think
about it is that it's

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.170
a conversational
interface, and it's

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.020
a conversational
interface between you

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.070
and Google that helps you get
things done in your world.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.150
The thing I'm really excited
about with the Assistant

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:51.640
is it builds on a decade or
so of research and development

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.450
that Google has done in a
wide range of computer science

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:01.150
topics, certainly in search,
but also in natural language

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:05.110
understanding, intent
processing, speech recognition,

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:09.430
text to speech, a whole
host of these core advances

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.410
that Google has made.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:14.470
But what's really interesting is
the way we brought it together

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.650
in a really magical experience.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:18.460
But that's not all.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:20.590
What's important to
me as a developer

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:25.750
is that we've also built on
Google's history of platforms.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.900
If you think about it, Google
Search was our first platform.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.350
It was a platform that connected
publishers with readers.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.100
Then we have a platform
with YouTube today

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.340
that connects creatives
with their viewers.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:44.110
And a platform with Play that
connects application developers

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.670
with their users.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.680
And building on
that rich history

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.270
of platforms with Google,
we have Actions on Google.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.750
And Actions on Google is the
way you as developers can

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:57.940
extend the Google Assistant.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.910
It allows you to
connect with your users

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.400
wherever the Google
Assistant is,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.090
and it allows you to help users
get things done with whatever

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.030
service that you can offer.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.070
And it allows you
to innovate, really,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.920
in a brand new
space, a new paradigm

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.040
with the way that users are
interacting with computers.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.080
This gives you a
nice framework, a way

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.440
to experiment and innovate.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.930
We launched the
platform late last year,

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.510
and we couldn't be more
happy with the actions

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:32.420
that we've seen develop for it.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.620
There's hundreds of actions
out there doing all kinds

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.120
of really interesting things.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.770
And I hope that by
the end of this talk,

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.490
you'll be ready to build your
own action for the Google

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.530
Assistant.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.110
So let's talk about that action.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.619
Guillaume and I were
working on this knowing

00:03:50.619 --> 00:03:52.160
this conference was
coming up, and we

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.514
thought it would be great
to help users find their way

00:03:54.514 --> 00:03:55.430
around the conference.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:59.180
So we built an action that
helps users understand

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.030
what sessions are going
on in the conference

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.260
and how to get things done.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:07.160
So this is just our
demo application.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.610
The way it works with the
Assistant on Google Home is--

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.460
that's where we've launched
Actions on Google so far is

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.550
on Google Home--

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.870
is users give some sort of
query, like, OK, Google,

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:21.410
let me talk to Cloud Next '17.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:25.220
And then the Assistant
will respond,

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.590
sure, here's Cloud Next.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.780
And then you hear an earcon.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.750
Earcon is to the ear what
an icon is to the eye.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:37.310
That's a signification, a
demarcation that something

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.160
is changing in this dialogue.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.010
And then our action
actually kicks in

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:46.580
and takes over the conversation
and has a two-way dialogue

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:47.720
with the user.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.700
And that happens in
a different voice

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.630
to further differentiate
the experience from what's

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.430
going on with the user.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:58.230
So there are four
voices to choose from,

00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.960
and you can go and
hear audio clips--

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.865
two male and two female voices.

00:05:01.865 --> 00:05:04.760
So you can pick the one
that best matches what

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.670
you're trying to accomplish.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.140
Anyway, then there's
a two-way dialogue.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.500
And then when either party is
ready to end that conversation,

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:14.990
the conversation goes
back to the Assistant

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:18.710
after the exit earcon is played.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:20.720
So that's the high
level framework.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.430
What we're going
to do in this talk

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:24.320
is we're going to build
that action for you.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.440
We're going to walk
through the entire process.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.150
From the beginning,
we're going to talk

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:32.630
about designing for
this new modality,

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.550
for this eyes-free modality.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:37.820
It's a very different experience
than if you built a website

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.500
or an application before.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.370
Then we're going to talk
about the development of such

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:46.910
an application, both in
terms of the machine learning

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.060
natural language understanding
aspect as well as connecting

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.204
to your back-end systems and
hosting of your business logic.

00:05:53.204 --> 00:05:54.620
Then we're going
to talk about how

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.550
to deploy that action,
both in terms of the server

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.950
deployments that need to
happen as well as how you

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:03.770
help Google understand about
that action with Actions

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:05.810
on Google.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.570
I'm going to jump in and talk
about the design section.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.130
First we need to
have a conversation

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.170
about conversations.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:20.250
Will we ever be able to
talk to machines the way

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.530
we talk to people?

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:26.050
Science fiction certainly
says that we can.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:31.590
But let's be honest,
talking to computers

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.190
is really difficult today.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:36.480
If you've ever been through
one of the voice trees before,

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.250
you know that talking to
computers today really sucks.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:45.600
But that's only because
human-to-human communication

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.270
is only deceptively easy.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:52.980
It's nothing short of
miraculous that two people

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.670
can enter a conversation and
leave it knowing something

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.960
neither of them knew going in.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.230
So conversations
are easy, right?

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.420
Well, they are
easy for the user.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.530
In fact, they're
probably the easiest way

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.125
that users have to interact.

00:07:11.125 --> 00:07:12.500
You don't have to
guess what will

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.550
happen when I push a button, I
just use my natural language.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:21.230
But for us as developers
and software professionals,

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:22.160
it's hard.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.910
These rules are
hard to codify, it's

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:28.310
difficult to make a
computer really understand

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.350
what a user is saying.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:35.940
But it's only easy
for us as humans

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:39.240
because we've been doing
it our whole lives.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.300
Conversations are
governed by language,

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.020
and language has
rules, and language

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.930
has syntax and context.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:52.200
And as humans, we've
gotten amazingly good

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.740
at understanding and
dealing with context.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:04.290
But take heart, we don't
have to get there overnight.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.330
Humans have been
speaking in languages

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.340
for hundreds of
thousands of years,

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:11.910
and we've only just
started to have computers

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:13.740
trying to talk to humans.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.720
But this does mean
because humans

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:19.890
have been speaking this way
so long it's probably better

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.670
to teach computers to
talk like humans rather

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.170
than the other way around.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:28.330
So let's talk more concretely.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.110
How do we design
an interface that

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:37.460
helps humans talk to computers?

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.620
Well, I'm going to offer
a couple of tips for you.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:45.080
The first one is to
create a persona.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.230
The way you interact
with your wife or husband

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.600
is very different than
the way you interact

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.870
with your boss, that's different
than the way you interact

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.180
with a person selling
tickets, because there's

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.430
a different persona, different
roles involved there.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:01.370
So it's no wonder
that the first thing

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.980
you need to do when you build
your action for the Google

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.680
Assistant is to think
about what persona you're

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.860
trying to create.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.070
This is some rules
that we've processed,

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:15.830
that we've laid down, and we've
used it with many partners.

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:17.370
It seems to work.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:20.990
First, we try to identify
our core brand attributes.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:23.780
What is fundamental about
our company or a bot

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:26.690
that we're trying to do?

00:09:26.690 --> 00:09:29.570
When Guillaume and I started
working on this action,

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:33.290
we thought, we wanted it to
be knowledgeable, helpful,

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:36.110
encouraging, that's kind of the
experience we wanted out of it.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:40.700
Next, we tried to
tease out-- what

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.310
are those design principles?

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:45.854
What are the guide rails
that puts on our development?

00:09:45.854 --> 00:09:47.270
One of the partners
we worked with

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:49.070
told me this process
really helped

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:52.300
them trim features
from their experience

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.490
by deciding what the
design principles are.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.460
So for us, we really
wanted it to be data rich.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:02.870
We wanted it to consistently
recommend and be proactive.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:05.780
So actually, Guillaume
spent a bunch of time

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:08.660
getting all the data we
could about what sessions

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:11.720
were available, when they were
available, who was speaking,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:16.590
what rooms, so that we would
be a very data rich experience.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.890
Next, think about the
actual dialogue writing.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:20.940
What is the voice?

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.720
What's the style and
persona that we want?

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.730
For us, we thought,
maybe a little geeky--

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:29.490
I mean, this is still
a Google conference--

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:33.080
but eager and
motivating as well.

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:38.070
And finally, we recommend you
create a style guide or a bio

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:42.840
sketch that you can use to
inform the writing of dialogue,

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:45.180
a concrete artifact.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.470
Here is a template of one.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.960
This is the one that we used.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.600
Notice it says sample things
that your action might say.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.044
What might your action say?

00:10:56.044 --> 00:10:57.960
What are things that you
would just never say?

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.630
They're outside
of their persona,

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.350
you shouldn't say that.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:04.400
And then what words
should you use?

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.600
What are some substitute words?

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.750
What you find, especially if
you're doing this at scale,

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:12.390
is you may have several
people writing dialogue

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:15.210
for your action,
maybe the person

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:20.250
changes over time, as happens
in development processes.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.500
And having an artifact
like this that

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:28.260
really makes concrete who you're
trying to be as your action

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.290
can really help.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:32.090
That leads us to
the next one, which

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:33.710
is around writing dialogue.

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:36.380
So here's an actual
sample dialogue

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:38.480
that we started writing.

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:40.730
And then you can see
the mark up that we

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:42.920
did as we went
through and started

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:46.430
applying some of the design
principles that we have,

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:51.020
and that just gives you a sense
of how this process can go.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:53.090
That is really my
second tip is really

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:57.270
to go and invest the time to
write some sample dialogue.

00:11:57.270 --> 00:12:00.710
And that can be as simple as
opening up a Google Docs file

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:06.320
and writing dialogue using
the style guide as a way

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.110
to help yourself do that.

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:13.130
But there's a few samples
we suggest you write.

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.590
So first, write
down the happy path.

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:17.960
What's your dream path,
the way people are supposed

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:19.400
to go through your action?

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.120
And make sure
that's good and you

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:24.140
thought through what the
interactions with that are.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:27.440
Next, think about that
first time user experience.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.660
Somebody who's never
used it before,

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:33.380
what do you do to help them
understand the interaction so

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.090
that they know what you can do?

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:39.770
You frame the problem for them,
so that they ask questions

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:42.350
that you're more likely
to be able to answer.

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:45.410
Next is the opposite, which is
think about the returning user.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.750
Once you've earned
them back, how

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.720
can you trim down the experience
or taper the experience

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.810
so that they can get to
the functionality they

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:56.180
want quicker?

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:59.810
The next one is there are
no errors in dialogue.

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:01.160
There's never an error.

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.440
There's just
clarification of meaning.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:05.330
When we have a
conversation, sometimes we

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:08.150
misunderstand each other
and we repeat back,

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:10.280
we clarify, we
ask, did you mean,

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:12.890
we get more
information in context.

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:15.800
Before we take some major
action, maybe we confirm it.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:17.720
If it's not a major
action, maybe we

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:19.820
are confident enough
to just do it.

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:23.430
So thinking about
some repair scenarios.

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.190
And finally, you need to
think about those personality

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:27.090
questions.

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:29.520
What we've seen from
the Google Assistant

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.100
generally is, especially
when users are first

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.410
starting to use
your action, they

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.650
probe it and they ask it
some personality questions.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:39.125
"Hi, how are you?"

00:13:39.125 --> 00:13:39.780
"Thank you."

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.160
"Good evening."

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:42.510
"Do you want to go on a date?"

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:44.820
"Can I marry you?"

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.340
Those are questions
you've got to think about

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:48.720
within your persona--

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:50.425
what are appropriate answers?

00:13:50.425 --> 00:13:50.701
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
(IMPERSONATING ASSISTANT)

00:13:50.701 --> 00:13:51.550
Sorry, Brad.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:53.010
I don't want to marry you.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:54.160
BRAD ABRAMS: Yeah, you do--

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:54.760
we'll talk.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:55.320
We'll talk.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:00.864
So that's a bit on design.

00:14:00.864 --> 00:14:02.780
Let's turn it over and
talk about development.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.620
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Right.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:07.855
I'm a Developer Advocate, so
I'm also developing, obviously.

00:14:07.855 --> 00:14:12.030
So I'm happy to speak
about development.

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:14.460
So what's happening?

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:16.050
We are users.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:19.380
We're talking to our
Google Home device.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:24.900
The Google Home device is going
to interact with the Google

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:27.180
Assistant.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.150
We speak to the device.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:34.120
So there's speech
utterance that's

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:37.170
going to be transferred
to the Assistant.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.500
And we're going to do
speech-to-text analysis to be

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:44.750
able to think and react
to what's being said,

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:46.639
but with words,
which are easier--

00:14:46.639 --> 00:14:48.180
well, written words,
let's say, which

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:51.150
are easier to
understand than voice.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.870
We do the natural language
processing to extract things

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:59.070
like the entities or
the parameters that

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:03.210
make the conversation as well
as the intent of the user.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:09.040
The user wants to learn about a
particular session, et cetera.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:15.240
And there's lots of intelligence
in there, the ranking,

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.280
picking up who the
user actually is,

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:21.780
et cetera, as well as
text-to-speech back when

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:25.300
we go back to the user.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:29.750
So we ask to speak
to our assistant.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:34.260
So, sure, OK, here
is Google Next 2017.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:38.260
And in the back, it's
going to call API.AI,

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:42.360
which contains our
Assistant, the structure, all

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:43.875
the intents, all the entities.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:51.540
"Hi, welcome to Cloud
Next '17," and then

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:53.040
the conversation really starts.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:56.920
So, "I want to hear more
about machine learning."

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:00.300
So, again, speech recognition,
et cetera, going on,

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:02.260
speech-to-text.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:06.960
And it's going to invoke API.AI.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.410
There are some things which
can be done automatically

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:14.631
from within API.AI, let's say
canned responses and things

00:16:14.631 --> 00:16:15.130
like that.

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:17.880
But sometimes you actually
need to take some data

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:20.320
from somewhere else.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:23.310
And that's where we're going to
use the Google Cloud Platform,

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:26.520
and in particular
Cloud Functions,

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:30.990
which as you discovered this
morning was announced as Beta.

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.690
And that's within
Cloud Functions

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:37.770
that I'm going to write the
business logic of my action.

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:41.760
And what's nice is that
on the Next website,

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:44.250
there's actually
a REST API that I

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:48.060
can invoke to get the list of
speakers, the list of sessions,

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:50.590
the list of topics, et cetera.

00:16:50.590 --> 00:16:53.580
So my function is
going to invoke

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:59.610
that REST API to find all
the talks with the label

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:01.170
machine learning, basically.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.450
And then, "the next
merchandising session is,"

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:04.390
blah, blah, blah.

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:10.319
And then with text-to-speech I
get the response, the session

00:17:10.319 --> 00:17:12.690
I was interested in.

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:14.550
And I let Brad--

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:15.369
Oh, yeah, no.

00:17:15.369 --> 00:17:17.160
Let me just say a few
words about that one,

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.980
and I'll let you do
the demo afterwards.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.640
So API.AI, what is it?

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:25.290
So it's a cross-platform
tool for building

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:29.730
advanced conversational
interfaces.

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:33.690
We define intents,
so the different kind

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:38.010
of questions or interactions you
can have with your Assistant,

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:42.240
but then those interactions also
need sometimes some parameters.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:46.440
So one intent would
be, I'd like to learn

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:48.760
about machine learning, like
the example we had before.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.830
So the intent is
learning about something.

00:17:50.830 --> 00:17:54.510
But that something,
that's our parameter here.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:59.940
So it's exactly like mapping
utterance, what the user says

00:17:59.940 --> 00:18:01.740
to, actually, a function call.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:05.160
The function name would be
the intent that we try to call

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:07.290
and the parameters,
all the parameters,

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:10.680
like the topic machine learning,
that we pass as a parameter

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:12.450
to this intent.

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:15.310
And I'll let this time--

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:18.300
BRAD ABRAMS: Can
we have demo one?

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:23.760
So what you're seeing
is the API to AI tool,

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:26.730
and it's a tool that's
freely available.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.660
Everybody can go use it.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:32.320
And it's available right now.

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:34.650
It is the tool that
helps understand

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:36.960
this huge vagaries
of human language

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:41.460
and turns it into
intents with arguments.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:42.930
Here's the set of
intents I have.

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:44.263
We'll talk about it in a second.

00:18:44.263 --> 00:18:46.410
And then this is a little
test experience here.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:52.480
For example, we can say,
talk to Cloud Next '17.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:55.530
And that's an example
user utterance,

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:59.520
and then it gives us the same
result we saw on the slides.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:03.010
But what's more interesting is
it's actually taken that text

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:06.690
and it said, that goes with
this particular intent.

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:09.900
In this case, it's the
default welcome intent.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:13.920
And you can see in this
default welcome intent,

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:17.160
we've given several
user says expressions.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:18.660
We've given several
examples of what

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.810
a user might say that should
come into this intent.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:24.810
And then in this case,
we've given some static text

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:26.940
that you should respond.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:29.490
So that looks pretty
easy, like you could maybe

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:32.580
do that with an if-statement.

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:34.680
But it also works--

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.330
we do some basic
machine learning

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:41.430
so you can add words like
please and it still gets it.

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:49.167
Or can I get Cloud Next 2017?

00:19:49.167 --> 00:19:51.250
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: And
it's not a French keyboard.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:55.410
BRAD ABRAMS: Yes, not
even a French keyboard.

00:19:55.410 --> 00:19:58.260
So even if there's
some optionality

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:00.630
and it learns over time,
you can do some training

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:03.040
on this, a more advanced topic.

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:05.340
So that's pretty basic.

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:07.870
Actually, an aside here, I
was actually showing this

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:09.490
to one of the Cloud
Marketing guys

00:20:09.490 --> 00:20:12.730
and the prompt is, what
would you like to know?

00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:15.240
And they said, well, clearly
the thing I want to know

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:19.967
is where can I get a cocktail?

00:20:19.967 --> 00:20:21.300
That's what marketing guys want.

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:24.060
There's nobody from
Cloud Marketing here.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:25.560
That's what they
wanted to know is--

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:27.570
where can I get a cocktail?

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:31.560
And you can see we give
an OK response here.

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:35.670
It's the default help
intent that basically we

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.070
didn't know what to match
it to, so we match it

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:39.300
to the help intent.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:42.210
So that's kind of
not a great answer.

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:44.340
Let's actually see
what it would be,

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:46.710
since the marketing guys
are paying for this,

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:50.100
if we can help them
find their cocktail.

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.370
Let's call the
intent find-cocktail.

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:55.920
And then what a user might
say if they were looking

00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:01.230
for a cocktail, well, they might
say, where are the cocktails?

00:21:01.230 --> 00:21:03.750
Can I get a drink?

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:05.310
GUILLAUME LAFORGE:
Where's the booze?

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:07.320
BRAD ABRAMS: Yeah, exactly.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:10.200
Are there any cocktails?

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:13.800
So we recommend between 5
and 10 of these expressions

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:18.210
to get started to have
enough to have it make sense.

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:22.290
Then I worked with marketing
to get some approved language

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:26.760
for this action, because this
is the most important one,

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:28.000
clearly.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:30.120
And now when I give
that same query,

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:31.650
where can I get a cocktail?

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:35.400
Notice this time it matched
to the find-cocktails.

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:44.000
In fact, is there
a cocktail, please?

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.240
It still matches.

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:49.135
So same kind of optionality,
whatever, gets done.

00:21:49.135 --> 00:21:50.510
And if you do some
more training,

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:52.050
it can get even better.

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:53.170
That's pretty simple.

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:57.040
Let's look at something a
little bit more complex in this

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:59.760
find topics.

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:02.760
The way this one works is
we've given several samples,

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:07.170
but we've highlighted some
things as entities or slots.

00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:11.490
We're saying any time they
say breakout sessions,

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:15.150
whether they say big
data or another topic,

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:17.300
then it should
match this intent.

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:21.640
Or are there any talks, or I
want to know about, or whatnot.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:24.640
You can see this actually
matches to a topic.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:26.580
And the topic is
actually an entity,

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:28.470
so we flip over to this entity.

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.180
You can see that
we've actually pulled

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:33.900
in a lot of data about what
are all the entities, what

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:38.010
are all the things
that users might say.

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:41.490
For example, I believe we are
now in the big data machine

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:42.930
learning topic.

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:44.730
So we've said, look,
other ways people

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:49.230
might refer to that
topic are these aliases.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:58.920
So now, for example, if we say,
I need to learn about big data,

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:02.270
then it will match that,
hopefully, it'll match that

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:06.020
to the find topic intent.

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:06.680
That's great.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:08.900
And it actually
matched the topic

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:11.400
to big data and
machine learning.

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:12.710
So that's pretty cool.

00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:17.600
It actually matched it
and extracted that entity.

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:22.820
To make things more interesting,
I mentioned in the UX section

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:25.310
about context.

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:32.570
If I reset the context and
I say, talk to GCP Next,

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:35.079
and I say more here,
it says, sorry, I

00:23:35.079 --> 00:23:36.620
don't know what
you're talking about.

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:39.210
Because more doesn't make
sense for that prompt.

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:46.940
But if I ask about big
data, then, hopefully, it

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:51.570
matches to that
intent, and it does.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:54.440
Then here I can say, I want
to know more about that.

00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:57.110
And now more actually
has a meaning.

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:59.210
We're within a context.

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:00.650
So the way--

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:05.340
I'm sure this is going to
work in just a second, yes.

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:06.280
Yes.

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:08.120
Can we just try
it one more time?

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:10.560
Yeah, there it is.

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:12.330
The way it works--
this time it knew what

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:14.340
it was, and it got the data.

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:17.280
And notice the context
is, find-by-more-next.

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:20.790
And that's because we
actually set up this context.

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:24.930
When it leaves this intent,
it will set that context.

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:33.540
And then the more Next intent
is guarded by, it requires this

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:35.790
find-by-context-next.

00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:37.820
So that's the basics there.

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:45.630
One more time, here's big
data, and let's see what

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:47.740
the JSON actually looks like.

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:50.250
So you can see this is
actually what API.AI is

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:52.140
going to send to your web hook.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:55.680
So all API.AI does is
take the natural language

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:58.680
and turn it into a function
with a method call.

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:02.460
The function is this.

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:05.310
It says the action
is find-by-topic,

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:07.920
and the parameter here is
big-data-and-machine-learning.

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:10.800
So it's taken this enormous
variety of human language

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:16.380
and turned it into this
big-data-machine-learning

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:17.650
find-by-topic.

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:20.340
So now Guillaume is going
to take over and show you

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:22.020
how to process that JSON.

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.250
GUILLAUME LAFORGE: Exactly.

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:26.550
So let's go back to the slides.

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:30.310
I'm going to say a few
words about Cloud Functions.

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:35.250
So here, find-by-topics,
it's actually

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.140
going to call Cloud Function.

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:40.495
The cocktails, it was
already inside API.AI.

00:25:40.495 --> 00:25:43.050
It didn't have to get
outside of API.AI.

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:45.480
But here we're going
to use Cloud Functions

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:50.190
for doing the function call.

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:52.920
The thing is here I'm speaking
about Cloud Functions,

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:56.460
but what you need is
just somewhere to have

00:25:56.460 --> 00:26:01.440
an application deployed that's
able to parse and produce

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:02.760
JSON content, basically.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:05.737
So it doesn't have to be
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:26:05.737 --> 00:26:07.320
It doesn't have to
be Cloud Functions.

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:09.990
You can use anything
anywhere as long

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:15.600
as it's a URL which is
available on the internet.

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:17.850
But why Cloud Functions?

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:22.680
It's been announced
as Beta this morning.

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:25.560
It's our new
serverless platform.

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:29.820
It's event-based
or event-oriented.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:35.670
There are three kinds of events
which are supported right now.

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:38.010
Cloud storage
updates, for example,

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:41.580
imagine you have an
image which is uploaded

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:44.410
and then you want to
react to do the nails

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:47.670
and resizing or
applying some filters.

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:49.410
If you're using
Cloud Pub/Sub, it's

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:52.990
going to react to a new message
which appears on some topic.

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:57.060
But the one we're interested
in is the HTTP trigger.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:27:00.390
So you can trigger
a Cloud function

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:03.270
via a call, an HTTP call.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:10.330
In our case, that's a post
request to our Cloud function.

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:16.440
So what's interesting with that
Cloud Function, especially when

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:19.920
you're writing something
like an Assistant,

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:24.360
you're not necessarily
a super wizard

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:30.060
with tons of knowledge of
how to deal with your servers

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.770
and things like that.

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:35.055
That's what's nice with
a serverless approach.

00:27:35.055 --> 00:27:37.590
And with Cloud Functions
being a fully managed service,

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:40.560
you really don't have
to care and worry

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.020
about the ops side of things.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:44.940
Also, the other
nice aspect of it

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:48.670
is that there's automatic
scaling happening.

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:53.910
So even if your Assistant
is very successful,

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:56.130
you won't have a
problem with scaling

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:58.282
because the scaling
is done automatically.

00:27:58.282 --> 00:27:59.490
It's a fully managed service.

00:27:59.490 --> 00:28:02.280
And it's also cost
effective, because it's not

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:06.540
like long running
VMs or containers.

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:09.170
It's built by basically
the function call.

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:12.150
I don't remember the billing
details for a Cloud Function,

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:14.160
but it's pretty cost effective.

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:17.140
If you don't get anyone
using your agent,

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:18.900
it won't cost you anything.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:21.240
And the last aspect, which
is important as well,

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:25.380
is that Cloud Functions, when
you offer Cloud Functions,

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:26.940
you develop Cloud
Functions, it's

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:29.730
using JavaScript and Node.js.

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:33.180
So pretty much any
developer these days

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:38.550
know enough of JavaScript to be
able to write Cloud Functions.

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.670
Even if you're a
front-end developer,

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:45.465
you're obviously using
JavaScript at some point.

00:28:45.465 --> 00:28:47.340
So you don't have to be
a back-end developer.

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:49.381
But if you're a back-end
developer using Node.js,

00:28:49.381 --> 00:28:52.290
well, fine.

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:55.660
That was also the
announcement of the [? GF ?]

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:57.660
App Engine Flex, by the way.

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:00.425
And there's Node.js
support in App Engine Flex,

00:29:00.425 --> 00:29:01.550
so you could also use that.

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:08.040
So let's get back to our
little architecture diagram.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:11.850
So we have the user
asking the home device,

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:16.200
contacting the Assistant,
then calling API.AI.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:18.485
Then for some of
the intents, it's

00:29:18.485 --> 00:29:19.860
calling our Cloud
Function, which

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:25.990
calls the REST API exposed
on the Next websites.

00:29:25.990 --> 00:29:29.670
But before saying
that, Cloud Function

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:31.620
is really pretty nifty.

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:35.160
When you deploy a new
version of a function,

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:39.180
you're developing, OK, let's
answer somewhat differently.

00:29:39.180 --> 00:29:44.990
Or let's see, there's a
special case in the dialogue

00:29:44.990 --> 00:29:47.870
that I need to
handle, like an error,

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:51.930
because the next REST API
is not available, et cetera.

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:54.410
So you are developing,
and then you

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:57.690
have to redeploy your function.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:29:59.470
Deploying a function
is pretty fast.

00:29:59.470 --> 00:30:02.300
It takes 20 or 30 seconds.

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.100
But when you are
developing, you would

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:06.900
like to have somewhat of
a tighter feedback loop

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:12.190
and be able to develop more
quickly without having to wait,

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:13.989
let's say, 30 seconds
every five minutes.

00:30:13.989 --> 00:30:15.530
So you're good for
five minutes, then

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:19.250
you wait to get 30 seconds
where you deploy your changes.

00:30:19.250 --> 00:30:20.930
So it can be a little
bit irritating.

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:25.610
So I'm going to tell you about
two interesting tools which

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:31.850
allow you to be more productive
and see your changes live.

00:30:31.850 --> 00:30:33.560
So the two tools I'm
going to talk about

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:39.115
are ngrok and the Cloud
Functions Emulator.

00:30:39.115 --> 00:30:41.780
The Cloud Functions
Emulator is a way

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:45.840
to actually run Cloud Functions
locally on your machine,

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:48.200
on localhost, basically.

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:50.210
It's a Node model.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:51.170
It's open source.

00:30:51.170 --> 00:30:54.020
It's available on GitHub.

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:56.910
You can use pretty
much the same commands

00:30:56.910 --> 00:31:02.120
as when you use G Cloud
Functions, deploy, et cetera.

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.010
It's pretty much the
same set of functions

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:07.760
which are available-- of
commands which are available.

00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:10.850
But there are two nice aspects--

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:11.840
live reloading.

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:15.170
So as soon as you make
changes to your code,

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:16.966
the changes are
taken into account.

00:31:16.966 --> 00:31:18.590
So you don't have to
redeploy anything.

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:21.110
The changes are live right away.

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:24.560
And the other thing that I
will not cover in my demo

00:31:24.560 --> 00:31:28.310
is the fact that you can
also debug in Chrome--

00:31:28.310 --> 00:31:33.560
what you are doing to your
code, the body of your function.

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:38.390
Which can be pretty handy
because it's not like putting

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:41.390
println() statements
when you interact.

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:45.620
Or the println() statements is
like talking through the Google

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:47.780
Home device, basically.

00:31:47.780 --> 00:31:49.940
And the other tool--

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:52.470
the Emulator is working
locally on your machine.

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:55.130
So it's, as I
said, on localhost.

00:31:55.130 --> 00:32:00.530
But API.AI, when you say, use
my webhook pointing at my Cloud

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:07.140
Function, it needs a URL which
is accessible from the web.

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:10.940
Well, localhost is usually
not accessible from the web.

00:32:10.940 --> 00:32:15.860
Otherwise, your machine might
perhaps be hacked or something.

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:17.660
But there's this
nifty little tool

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:21.200
called ngrok, which
is both a service

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:24.260
and a come online
tool, which creates

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:29.280
a secure tunnel over the
internet to your machine.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:32.620
So let's see that in action.

